    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the coordinates of point \\( A = (4, 1) \\) after translating by 2 units to the right and 3 units up.\n2. Determine the coordinates of point \\( B = (-8, 5) \\) after translating by 2 units to the right and 3 units up.\n3. Find the midpoint of the new endpoints \\( A' \\) and \\( B' \\).\n\nFirst, let's translate the points by 2 units to the right and 3 units up.\n\nThe initial coordinates of points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are given as \\( (4,1) \\) and \\( (-8,5) \\), respectively. We add 2 units to the \\( x \\)-coordinate and 3 units to the \\( y \\)-coordinate for each point.\n\n\\[\nA' = (4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n\\]\n\\[\nB' = (-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the midpoint of \\( A' = (6, 4) \\) and \\( B' = (-6, 8) \\). The formula for the midpoint of two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of \\( A' \\) and \\( B' \\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
